                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-25",
                                    "linecount": 46,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 683.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nvu_ppm_meter_env — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA level-meter movement, not a fast detector: the rectified tuba is averaged\nwith an attack/release time constant and that averaged level drives a damped\nsecond-order needle. The needle overshoots slightly and settles, which is the\nmechanical part a digital one-pole cannot give. At 300/300 ms it is a slow\nVU-like body reading; at 10/1500 ms it is the PPM attack/decay.\n\nSource: Dutilleux, DAFx-98, §5.3-5.4 (VU = AM detector; PPM = full-wave\nrectifier + 10 ms/1500 ms AR-averager, DIN 45406); esskov, DAFx-07, §2.1\n(VU vs PPM ballistics, mechanical needle).\n\nPARAMETERS (5)\n\n1. attack_ms — 5…500 ms, default 300 ms\n   Integration time for a rising level (63% point). 300 ms is the slow VU\n   body; 10 ms is the PPM rise.\n\n2. release_ms — 5…3000 ms, default 300 ms\n   Decay time. Keep it equal to attack_ms for VU-style symmetric ballistics;\n   1500 ms with a 10 ms attack is the PPM.\n\n3. needle_hz — 5…30 Hz, default 12 Hz\n   Resonance of the needle movement. Low values give a heavy, slow meter that\n   lags and overshoots; high values approach a plain smoothed envelope.\n\n4. damping — 0.3…1.5, default 0.7\n   Needle damping (zeta). Below 1 the needle overshoots and rings briefly on an\n   attack; 1 is critically damped; above 1 is sluggish.\n\n5. out_db — -24…+24 dB, default 0 dB\n   Level of the control signal on out1.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1: tuba audio (mono).\n- out1: needle deflection, 0..1 before out_db, at audio rate.\n\nNOTES\n- attack_ms/release_ms here are 63% times, not the standards' 99% pointer\n  response, so 300 ms is a musical VU-like weight rather than a calibration.\n- A resonant needle can overshoot slightly above 1 on a hard attack; that is\n  the movement, not a limiter fault.\n- No safety limiter, no metering, no gate.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
