                                    "text": "param out_db 0 @min -24 @max 24",
                                    "varname": "out_db"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-25",
                                    "linecount": 50,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 741.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nvactrol_opto_env — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nThe envelope of a vactrol: rectified tuba drives a LED, the light falls on a CdS\nphotoresistor, and the cell's conductance is the output. It attacks faster as\nthe LED gets brighter and releases slowly; after a sustained note the cell is\nstill \"charged\", so the release stretches further. That program dependence is\nwhat makes an opto compressor breathe instead of clamp, and it is the point of\nthis follower.\n\nSource: Najnudel, Müller, Hélie & Roze, DAFx-23, paper 50, §1, §3 Eqs. 3-4,\n§5 (VTL5C3/2). This is a reduced signal-level model of the published physical\nmodel, chosen to run per-sample in codebox~.\n\nPARAMETERS (5)\n\n1. sensitivity_db — -24…+24 dB, default 0 dB\n   Trim into the LED. Higher values light the cell earlier and make the attack\n   faster; it also changes where the gamma curve sits, so it is a drive control,\n   not just a volume trim.\n\n2. release_ms — 20…2000 ms, default 300 ms\n   Dark decay of the photoresistor at low exposure. The classic opto tail is a\n   few hundred ms; 1-2 s is a slow, sustained breathing envelope.\n\n3. program — 0…1, default 0.6\n   How strongly recent light stretches the release (0 = fixed release, 1 = up\n   to 5x longer after a sustained bright note). This is the memory that makes\n   the cell lag a repeated riff differently on the first and later notes.\n\n4. curve — 0.5…1.5, default 0.8\n   LDR gamma: output = light^gamma. Below 1 the cell is more sensitive at low\n   levels (more output for a quiet note); above 1 it stays darker longer and\n   then opens quickly.\n\n5. out_db — -24…+24 dB, default 0 dB\n   Level of the control signal on out1.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1: tuba audio (mono).\n- out1: normalized LDR conductance, 0..1 before out_db, at audio rate.\n\nNOTES\n- 0 is the dark cell and 1 is full light; it is conductance, not resistance, so\n  a brighter note gives a larger output (a raw R_LDR would move the other way).\n- The attack is intentionally not brick-wall smooth: the cell follows the\n  waveform a little, which is part of the opto character.\n- No safety limiter, no metering, no gate.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
