                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-13",
                                    "linecount": 47,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 698.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nhilbert_envelope — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nThe amplitude of the analytic signal: a quadrature allpass pair makes the real\nand 90-degree-shifted copies of the tuba, and sqrt(re^2 + im^2) is the\ninstantaneous amplitude. There is no rectifier, no threshold and no release —\nthe reading follows the waveform's own amplitude, so it is the fastest and most\ntransient-accurate envelope in the batch. A short one-pole after the magnitude\nsmooths the reading.\n\nImportant: this is the exact envelope of a narrowband signal, not a loudness\nmeter. A tuba is a harmonic stack, so |analytic| also contains the waveform's\nintra-cycle shape; on a 30-60 Hz note it will visibly follow that shape. That\nis the algorithm working as defined, not a bug. Raise smooth_ms (30-50 ms) for\na loudness-like contour, or use peak_ar_follower / rms_true_follower /\ncascade_env_follower when you want a smooth loudness envelope.\n\nSource: Dutilleux, DAFx-98, §5.1 (instantaneous envelope detector as the\nmagnitude of the analytic signal), §5.4 (no averager = finest resolution).\n\nPARAMETERS (2)\n\n1. smooth_ms — 0.5…50 ms, default 10 ms\n   Smoothing after the analytic magnitude. For a pure sine, 10 ms leaves about\n   3.2% ripple at 30 Hz and under 0.1% above 55 Hz. On a real tuba the harmonic\n   stack dominates the low-note ripple: 10 ms still shows the waveform shape at\n   30 Hz, 30-50 ms gives a loudness-like contour, and 2-5 ms keeps the most\n   transient detail at the cost of a visibly waveform-shaped envelope.\n\n2. out_db — -24…+24 dB, default 0 dB\n   Level of the control signal on out1. The analytic amplitude of a sine is its\n   peak amplitude, not its RMS.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1: tuba audio (mono).\n- out1: analytic amplitude, 0..1 before out_db, at audio rate.\n\nNOTES\n- The allpass recursion is y = c*(x + y1) - x1, H(z) = (c - z^-1)/(1 - c z^-1).\n  That is the pole-at-c form the cR/cI coefficients were fitted for; the other\n  allpass sign convention is not in quadrature with them.\n- The coefficients assume 44.1/48 kHz. At 96 kHz the quadrature drifts at the\n  bottom of the tuba range, so run this at 44.1 or 48 kHz.\n- The output is a control signal, not a monitor signal.\n- No safety limiter, no metering, no gate.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
