                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-21",
                                    "linecount": 46,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 683.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\ncascade_env_follower — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA peak envelope smoothed by several attack/release one-poles in series instead\nof one. Each stage starts from the previous stage's output, so the envelope's\nslope is continuous at the attack-to-release switch: the control signal sounds\nsmooth rather than steppy when it drives gain, cutoff or pitch. The stage\ncoefficients are normalized by the composite step time, so attack_ms and\nrelease_ms mean the same thing they do on the single-stage follower even when\nyou change the order.\n\nSource: Sanfilippo, SMC-22, §2.2 Eqs. 2-4 (cascaded exponential smoothers),\n§2.4 (overshoot/THD).\n\nPARAMETERS (4)\n\n1. attack_ms — 0.1…100 ms, default 5 ms\n   Composite 63% attack time of the whole cascade. Individual stages run faster\n   by q_M (4.35 at 4 stages, 8.64 at 8 stages) so the sum still lands here.\n\n2. release_ms — 5…2000 ms, default 300 ms\n   Composite 63% release time. Long values give a sustained body; short values\n   let the envelope breathe with the valve rhythm.\n\n3. stages — 2…8, default 4\n   Cascade order. 2 is close to a plain AR follower; 8 is the smoothest and\n   costs the most (one extra one-pole per stage per sample). 4 is the useful\n   middle for audio-rate modulation.\n\n4. out_db — -24…+24 dB, default 0 dB\n   Level of the control signal on out1. 0 dB leaves the natural 0..1 range.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1: tuba audio (mono).\n- out1: envelope control signal, 0..1 linear before out_db, at audio rate.\n\nNOTES\n- More stages soften the attack corner and add a little group delay, but the\n  63% timing stays at attack_ms/release_ms by construction.\n- The paper's C_M cutoff normalization is replaced here by a step-time\n  normalization q_M, because this effect is a control-signal follower where\n  timing, not the -3 dB corner, is what the knobs promise.\n- The output is a control signal, not a monitor signal.\n- No safety limiter, no metering, no gate.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
